BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Mosquers Film Festival
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260917
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260921
DTSTAMP:20260809T022900Z
UID:d62be336c25d62725172d0f3c0f4741b@explore-edmonton-calendar
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://assets.exploreedmonton.com/images/image-
 1.png
DESCRIPTION:Celebrating diverse Muslim experiences through film.\n\nThe Mo
 squers Film Festival is a nonprofit cultural organization based in Edmonto
 n\, Canada\, with a mission to transform the Muslim narrative through the 
 power of film\, art\, and storytelling. Founded in 2006\, The Mosquers beg
 an as a grassroots initiative - an intimate short film competition aimed a
 t tackling stereotypes and celebrating the diversity within Muslim communi
 ties. What started locally has since grown into the world’s leading film
  and arts festival dedicated to showcasing the breadth of Muslim experienc
 es. It now attracts filmmakers\, artists\, performers\, and audiences from
  around the world\, all united by a shared vision: to change how Muslims a
 re seen. The festival’s name - "Mosquers" - is a creative combination of
  "Mosque" and "Oscars\," representing a space that celebrates Muslim creat
 ivity\, identity\, and excellence in storytelling. Rooted in Muslim commun
 ity values\, The Mosquers is a home for voices often underrepresented in m
 ainstream media. Every September in Edmonton\, The Mosquers hosts a multi-
 day festival that includes an industry panel and networking event\, red-ca
 rpet gala\, the screening of short-film finalists\, a feature entertainmen
 t showcase\, a prestigious awards ceremony and end of show after-party. Pa
 st performers have included renowned Muslim creatives including Nemahsis\,
  Muna Abdulahi\, Malik El-Essal\, Hassan Phills\, Maz Jobrani\, Mo Amer\, 
 Narcy\, Azhar Usman\, Brother Ali and Mona Haydar. The Mosquers is about m
 ore than just film. It’s a cultural gathering that fosters open dialogue
 \, challenges misconceptions\, and creates space for connection. Each year
 \, we continue to introduce both Muslim and non-Muslim audiences to origin
 al\, authentic storytelling that entertains\, educates\, and inspires.\n\n
 Your guide to celebrating National Indigenous History Month in Edmonton\n\
 nEVENT INFORMATION\nLocation: The Winspear Centre - 4 Sir Winston Churchil
 l Square Northwest - Corner of 99 Street & 102 Avenue\, Edmonton\, Alberta
 \, CA\nVisit / Tickets: http://www.themosquers.com/\nExplore Edmonton: htt
 ps://exploreedmonton.com/event-calendar/mosquers-film-festival-2026
LAST-MODIFIED:20260809T022846Z
LOCATION:The Winspear Centre - 4 Sir Winston Churchill Square Northwest - 
 Corner of 99 Street & 102 Avenue\, Edmonton\, Alberta\, CA
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
URL:https://exploreedmonton.com/event-calendar/mosquers-film-festival-2026
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>Celebrating diverse Muslim experiences thr
 ough film.</p><p>The Mosquers Film Festival is a nonprofit cultural organi
 zation based in Edmonton\, Canada\, with a mission to transform the Muslim
  narrative through the power of film\, art\, and storytelling. Founded in 
 2006\, The Mosquers began as a grassroots initiative - an intimate short f
 ilm competition aimed at tackling stereotypes and celebrating the diversit
 y within Muslim communities. What started locally has since grown into the
  world’s leading film and arts festival dedicated to showcasing the brea
 dth of Muslim experiences. It now attracts filmmakers\, artists\, performe
 rs\, and audiences from around the world\, all united by a shared vision: 
 to change how Muslims are seen. The festival’s name - &quot\;Mosquers&qu
 ot\; - is a creative combination of &quot\;Mosque&quot\; and &quot\;Oscars
 \,&quot\; representing a space that celebrates Muslim creativity\, identit
 y\, and excellence in storytelling. Rooted in Muslim community values\, Th
 e Mosquers is a home for voices often underrepresented in mainstream media
 . Every September in Edmonton\, The Mosquers hosts a multi-day festival th
 at includes an industry panel and networking event\, red-carpet gala\, the
  screening of short-film finalists\, a feature entertainment showcase\, a 
 prestigious awards ceremony and end of show after-party. Past performers h
 ave included renowned Muslim creatives including Nemahsis\, Muna Abdulahi\
 , Malik El-Essal\, Hassan Phills\, Maz Jobrani\, Mo Amer\, Narcy\, Azhar U
 sman\, Brother Ali and Mona Haydar. The Mosquers is about more than just f
 ilm. It’s a cultural gathering that fosters open dialogue\, challenges m
 isconceptions\, and creates space for connection. Each year\, we continue 
 to introduce both Muslim and non-Muslim audiences to original\, authentic 
 storytelling that entertains\, educates\, and inspires.</p><p>Your guide t
 o celebrating National Indigenous History Month in Edmonton</p><h3>Event I
 nformation</h3><ul><li><strong>Location:</strong> The Winspear Centre - 4 
 Sir Winston Churchill Square Northwest - Corner of 99 Street &amp\; 102 Av
 enue\, Edmonton\, Alberta\, CA</li><li><strong>Visit / Tickets:</strong> h
 ttp://www.themosquers.com/</li><li><strong>Explore Edmonton:</strong> http
